;; claimgraph — open work, as an EDN map.
;;
;; Each entry carries the measurement or verbatim output that motivated it,
;; because the numbers are the expensive part to re-derive. Measurements were
;; taken on 2026-08-07/08 against this repo (88 source files, 640 code facts,
;; 11 pile files) on an M-series Mac with the Datalevin pod.
;;
;; Two larger pieces of work live in their own files, because each is an epic
;; with its own evidence and task breakdown rather than a single issue:
;;
;;   lazy-rewrite.edn            — shift from eager pre-assembly to
;;                                 just-in-time construction
;;   grep-baseline-benchmark.edn — does the graph beat grep on time, cost and
;;                                 accuracy? decides whether on-demand
;;                                 retrieval should exist at all
;;
;; Read with: (clojure.edn/read-string (slurp "issues.edn"))

{:unknown-flags-and-free-help
 {:title "Unknown flags are silently accepted, and --help starts a paid run"
  :priority :p0
  :size :small
  :description
  "The CLI accepts any unrecognized flag and proceeds as if it were not there.
   `claim audit --totally-bogus-flag` runs a normal audit and exits 0. Because
   `help` is a verb rather than a flag in this CLI, `claim audit --help` parses
   as an unknown boolean option and starts a REAL audit against the live
   extractor — several minutes of wall clock and real money, from the single
   most likely thing a new user types.

   `--help` should work on every verb, print that verb's usage, cost nothing,
   and exit 0. Unknown flags should exit 2, which is the code the top-level
   help already promises for `a flag value that would not parse`."
  :evidence
  ["claim audit --no-llm --totally-bogus-flag  =>  full normal scorecard, exit 0"
   "claim audit --help  =>  started a live audit; killed at ~150s after a preflight and extraction calls"]
  :approach
  "Declare flags per verb (the spec map already exists in cli.clj) and reject
   anything not in it. Add --help/-h as a universal flag handled before any
   work is dispatched, so it can never reach a code path that spends money.
   Consider a general rule: no verb may make a model call before argument
   validation completes."
  :notes
  "Found the hard way — a subagent typed `--help` while working on the audit
   pipeline and spent real tokens on an audit nobody asked for."}

 :admission-dedup
 {:title "Near-match deduplication at admission for entities and facts"
  :priority :p0
  :size :large
  :description
  "Paraphrases of one idea enter the graph as distinct facts, then surface as
   a combinatorial pile of conflicts demanding human adjudication. Six
   near-identical `reproduce-first review` claims, written by six different
   sessions, produced 15 pairwise conflicts — every one of them asking the
   user to resolve a disagreement that does not exist.

   This is the failure mode that decides whether the tool is worth using. The
   review queue IS the product; once it fills with restatements, users stop
   reading it, and every downstream feature inherits worthless input.

   Clustering the rendered view (shipped 2026-08-07) makes the symptom 83%
   cheaper to display. It does not stop the graph accumulating paraphrases,
   and the clustered line still reads oddly: `6 claims in contention` followed
   by six statements that say the same thing.

   Entities need the same treatment — `near-match` exists for entity lookup
   and self-heals by recording the queried name as an alias, but nothing
   applies that similarity at write time."
  :evidence
  ["6 paraphrase claims -> 15 conflict pairs -> 5047 bytes, 19% of the 25 KB injection window"
   "After display clustering: 1 line, 753 bytes (82.7% reduction) — symptom only"
   "Live store conflicts section: 16 rows, 15 of them one semantic disagreement"]
  :approach
  "Screen at admission rather than at render. `logic/screen-candidates` is the
   natural seam — it already runs before assert. Candidate mechanisms, cheapest
   first: normalized-object hashing for exact restatements; a similarity
   threshold over (subject, predicate) siblings; a judge call reserved for
   pairs above the threshold, which folds into the existing budget discipline.
   A near-duplicate should reinforce the standing fact (raising confidence,
   re-sourcing per the existing reinforcement rules) rather than mint a rival."
  :constraints
  ["Must not silently merge genuine disagreements — the 2-claim instruction-files
    conflict in the same section IS real and must survive."
   "Reinforcement already has re-sourcing semantics (spec/claims.allium); a merge
    should reuse them rather than invent a second path."
   "Any model call here must draw from the shared run budget, never a private one."]
  :see-also
  "grep-baseline-benchmark.edn :b6 — retrieval currently loses to grep partly
   because a query returns six paraphrases instead of one fact, so the
   benchmark run that informs the architecture decision must come after this."}

 :budget-allocation
 {:title "Budget allocation is unmanaged: stages starve, and a run cannot be predicted"
  :priority :p0
  :size :medium
  :description
  "Three related defects in how a run plans and divides its spend.

   1. NO PER-STAGE RESERVATION. Extraction, sweep, and judge draw from one flat
      pool in that order. On a default-budget run the judge — described in help
      as the false-positive filter that `keeps the headline honest` — never
      executes at all. Latency was hiding this: nobody waited 23 minutes to
      discover the last stage got nothing.

   2. NO PREFLIGHT ESTIMATE. Everything needed to predict a run is known before
      a single call is spent: the pile file count and their sizes, the code
      file count, the configured budget and concurrency, and an observed
      per-call latency. The user should see `11 files, ~20 calls, est. 3-4 min`
      and be able to adjust budget or concurrency BEFORE committing, rather
      than discovering the shape of the run by waiting it out.

   3. CALLS ARE A POOR UNIT. A 20 KB extraction and a one-word preflight both
      count as one. Allocation across stages in particular wants tokens, since
      extraction and judging have very different per-call sizes.

   Deferral is currently the only feedback, and it arrives after the money is
   spent: a run reports 42 deferred calls once there is nothing to be done."
  :evidence
  ["Real run, default budget 20: extraction 11 calls, sweep 9 calls, judge 0 calls, 42 deferred"
   "Output: `sweep: 9 candidates, 0 linked` then `judge: 0 verdicts, 0 compatible removed`"
   "llm-gate counts calls only: `llm: 20 calls, 1 failed, 42 deferred (budget 20)`"]
  :approach
  "Reserve a share per stage rather than serving first-come; the judge is the
   cheapest way to keep the headline honest, so it should be the last thing
   starved rather than the first. Derive an estimate from the scanned source
   list before the first call and print it (with a --dry-run that stops there).
   Revisit the default budget of 20, chosen when 20 calls meant 23 minutes of
   wall clock and now the binding constraint on coverage rather than time."
  :decided
  "Kira, 2026-08-08: budget allocation is a general problem, not just judge
   starvation; runtime should be estimable from the pile file count up front."
  :see-also [:cost-transparency :concurrency-timeout]}

 :batched-writes
 {:title "Batch the write path: 10.5ms fixed per commit, 125x available"
  :priority :p1
  :size :large
  :description
  "Store-backed ingest costs ~26ms per fact, essentially all of it commit
   overhead. Datalevin's transact cost is fixed per call (LMDB commit), not
   proportional to datom count, and each assert performs several: ensure-entity
   for the subject, sometimes the object, then the fact itself.

   This is the only thing in the system that scales with repository size. A
   10k-file repo at ~70k facts projects to roughly half an hour of pure writes.

   `core/ingest` documents per-fact transactions as deliberate: `applying
   conflict policy fact-by-fact takes precedence over batch atomicity`. That
   tradeoff is correct for facts that actually collide and vacuous for the rest.
   Crucially, the profile shows reads are three orders of magnitude cheaper than
   commits — so DECISIONS can stay strictly per-fact while only COMMITS batch."
  :evidence
  ["300 rows, one transact each: 3161.6 ms (10.54 ms/row)"
   "300 rows, single transact:   25.3 ms (0.08 ms/row)  => 125x"
   "resolve-entity 0.44 ms/call, get-facts 0.69 ms/call, -list-entities 1.58 ms/call"
   "cold insert 38.48 ms/fact; re-assert (duplicate path) 11.24 ms/fact"
   "claim ingest-code into a fresh store: 16.6s for 640 facts / 88 files"
   "the same 88 files through the in-memory audit store: 1.4s"]
  :approach
  "DECIDE AGAINST AN OVERLAY, FLUSH ONCE. Prefetch the slice the incoming batch
   touches — entities for the subject set, stable predicates, currently-valid
   facts for those subjects — run the unmodified decision loop against an
   in-memory projection seeded from it, and flush the recorded delta in one
   transact. Each decision sees every prior decision's effect, so conflict
   detection, supersession, reinforcement and flagging all batch too, not just
   the non-interacting share.

   The usual objection to an overlay is that it means reimplementing the
   store's mutation semantics in a second place, where the two can drift.
   That objection does not apply here: `claimgraph.store.memory` is already a
   full second implementation of the store protocol, and `bb test` runs the
   entire suite against BOTH backends. The equivalence is already asserted
   continuously, so the overlay reuses proven semantics rather than inventing
   them.

   `code/ingest!` already prefetches much of the needed slice for staleness
   diffing (`-select-facts` with :valid-cheap over the scope), so the read side
   is partly built.

   Cheaper first slice if the full overlay is deferred: pre-create all needed
   entities in one transact before the fact loop. Cold inserts create ~1.1
   entities per fact, so this alone removes most of the transacts."
  :constraints
  ["The oplog is the replication record and must keep per-fact granularity even
    when the store transact is batched — decouple oplog append from store commit."
   "Prefetch must be keyed by the incoming subject set, not the whole graph, or
    a large store trades write time for read time and memory."
   "Read-then-write widens the window another writer could change the store in.
    Single-writer is already the assumption (the curator is a singleton via
    try-acquire on <db>.curate.lock); decide whether the batch takes the lease."
   "Losing a batch to a crash loses more work than losing one fact. The oplog is
    the record and the store its materialized view, so recovery exists — confirm
    it actually replays."
   "Alias clash refusal is a write-time decision; it must fire in the overlay."
   "Format version stays 1; additive data only."
   "Verify against a worktree of the previous release in both directions —
    the standing rule for anything touching a persisted format."]
  :decided
  "Kira, 2026-08-08: batch the conflict decisions too, deciding against current
   db state plus accumulated in-memory changes, rather than only batching facts
   that provably cannot interact."}

 :doctor
 {:title "Health check, and surfacing silent background failure"
  :priority :p1
  :size :medium
  :description
  "The SessionEnd hook detaches `claim curate` and never looks back. If it
   fails every time — expired auth, exhausted budget, the recursive-hook bug
   that already happened once — nothing ever tells the user. A tool that writes
   to your memory unattended must be able to answer `is this working?` in one
   command, and must volunteer the answer when it is not.

   `claim stats` reports store counts. There is no health surface.

   Two halves, and both are needed: a `claim doctor` that reports on demand,
   and proactive surfacing so the next interactive command mentions a
   persistent background failure without being asked."
  :evidence
  ["Known prior incident: claude -p fired SessionEnd recursively, leaving consolidation
    permanently due; discovered by investigation, not by the tool reporting it."]
  :approach
  "Record a durable health row per background run (last success, last failure,
   consecutive failure count, last error). `doctor` reads it alongside the
   live checks setup already performs — dtlv present, extractor authenticated,
   store readable and format-compatible, lease not stale, hook wired to the
   binary that actually exists. Surface a persistent-failure banner on the next
   ordinary command, rate-limited so it informs rather than nags."}

 :episode-rollback
 {:title "Drop or roll back a session's ingest"
  :priority :p1
  :size :medium
  :description
  "`claim episode` offers open, close, and list. There is no way to retract one.

   When a session ingests garbage — a bad extraction run, a misconfigured
   source, an experiment — the user needs to undo that specific contribution
   without hand-editing the store. This is distinct from invalidation-as-
   decision-change: the claim is not superseded by better knowledge, it should
   never have been admitted.

   Structurally this is already available and simply unexposed: every fact
   carries its episode, and bi-temporality means retraction is an invalidation
   sweep that preserves history rather than a destructive delete."
  :evidence
  ["claim help: `episode open` / `episode close` / `episode list` — no retract verb"
   "A real run produced `claimgraph has-version llm-timeout-ms default 120s` from a
    misparse; today nothing removes that except manual invalidation."]
  :approach
  "A retraction verb taking an episode id, invalidating every fact it asserted
   with a distinct invalidation-kind that says `withdrawn` rather than
   `superseded` — so the Changed-recently briefing and the audit prongs can
   tell a mistake from a decision. Reinstate facts an ingest superseded, since
   an ingest that should not have happened should not have closed anything.
   Needs a dry-run showing exactly what would change."
  :constraints
  ["A withdrawn episode's effects may have been replicated; decide how the oplog
    carries a retraction to peers."
   "Facts reinforced by a later legitimate episode must not be withdrawn wholesale."]}

 :perf-regression-gate
 {:title "Performance budgets in CI, plus a soak suite"
  :priority :p1
  :size :medium
  :description
  "`bb bench` describes itself as doubling as a longitudinal regression check
   but asserts no time budgets, so nothing fails when throughput regresses. The
   26ms/fact write wall and the ~70s serial extraction both existed for a long
   time and were found by hand profiling, not by the suite.

   Beyond a gate, the system needs a soak suite: it is a long-lived, stateful,
   background-writing tool, and its interesting failures are accumulation
   failures — paraphrase pileup, non-converging curation, unbounded conflict
   growth, lease staleness. Those do not appear in a unit test that starts from
   an empty store."
  :evidence
  ["Wall found by manual profiling: 640 facts = 16.6s, entirely commit overhead"
   "Serial extraction: ~70s/call x 11 files, invisible to the suite"]
  :approach
  "Assert wall-clock budgets on a fixed corpus (`N facts in under X seconds`,
   generous enough not to flake on CI hardware but tight enough to catch an
   order of magnitude). For soak: drive many synthetic sessions against one
   store, then assert invariants over the result — conflicts do not grow
   without bound, curation converges, no episode stays open forever, the
   compiled view stays inside its budget, entity count tracks distinct subjects
   rather than drifting upward with paraphrase."}

 :cost-transparency
 {:title "Token-denominated budgets and cumulative cost reporting"
  :priority :p1
  :size :medium
  :description
  "Budgets are denominated in CALLS and scoped to a single run. Nothing
   accumulates across runs, and a call is a poor proxy for cost — extraction on
   a 20 KB file and a one-word preflight both count as one.

   For a tool making dozens of background model calls a day, `what did this
   cost me this week` is a precondition for people trusting it unattended."
  :evidence
  ["llm-gate counts calls only: `llm: 20 calls, 1 failed, 42 deferred (budget 20)`"
   "Preflight is one full round-trip and is deliberately unmetered"]
  :approach
  "Meter tokens where the extractor reports them, else estimate from prompt and
   reply bytes. Record per-run spend durably (the same place doctor reads).
   Report cumulative spend by day/week and by stage, so it is visible which
   stage the money goes to. Allow budgets expressed in tokens as well as calls."}

 :extraction-quality-gate
 {:title "Junk extractions are admitted and reported as findings"
  :priority :p1
  :size :medium
  :description
  "A real run extracted `claimgraph has-version` with the object `llm-timeout-ms
   default 120s` — the model read the phrase `120s llm-timeout-ms default` in a
   note as a version string. That fact was admitted, then surfaced to the user
   as a `disagreement` against the genuine version, carrying the same weight as
   a real finding.

   Distinct from near-duplicate admission: that is one idea entering many times,
   this is a bad parse entering once. Both erode the queue's signal, and the
   queue is the product."
  :evidence
  ["disagreement: claimgraph has-version 0.1.0-alpha  vs  claimgraph has-version llm-timeout-ms default 120s"
   "Real audit run, 107 claims from 11 files"]
  :approach
  "Predicates already declare object shape (value vs prose, 250 vs 1000 chars).
   Extend that vocabulary so a predicate can constrain its object beyond length
   — a version, a path, an identifier — and reject at screening rather than
   admitting and reporting. Track admitted-vs-rejected as a quality metric over
   time so prompt and model changes show up as movement rather than silence.
   Pairs naturally with a labelled fixture set as a regression gate on
   extraction precision."}

 :concurrency-timeout
 {:title "Parallel extraction pushes slow calls past the 120s timeout"
  :priority :p1
  :size :small
  :description
  "Six concurrent `claude -p` harnesses contend for CPU and memory, so per-call
   latency rises under parallelism. A call that comfortably fit the 120s
   default serially no longer does: a real run lost one file to
   llm-command-timeout.

   Introduced by the parallel-extraction change of 2026-08-08 and worth fixing
   deliberately rather than by lowering concurrency reflexively — the point of
   the design is that wall clock stops caring about any individual slow call."
  :evidence
  ["warning: .../allium-specs.md: extraction failed (llm-command-timeout)"
   "warning: 1 model call failed (llm-command-timeout: 1)"
   "Same file extracted fine under the serial path"]
  :approach
  "Scale the timeout with the configured concurrency, so contention cannot
   convert a slow call into a lost file. A lost file is a silent coverage hole:
   it reports as a warning and the pile is then audited without it, which reads
   as a clean result on an incomplete scan.

   Lowering concurrency instead would trade the whole point of the parallel
   design — wall clock no longer caring about any individual slow call — to
   defend a constant."
  :decided
  "Kira, 2026-08-08: raise the timeout with concurrency rather than capping
   concurrency."}

 :polish
 {:title "Small sharp edges found while measuring"
  :priority :p2
  :size :small
  :description
  "Collected minor items, each independently trivial:

   1. --concurrency is unclamped: `--concurrency 500` would fork 500 agent
      subprocesses. Clamp to something defensible.
   2. The audit scorecard prints absolute paths in its largest-injected line,
      which runs past 200 characters and wraps three times, burying the
      managed-view breakdown underneath. Relativize to the project root.
      DECIDED (Kira, 2026-08-08): relativizing is the mitigation — the line
      stays, the paths shorten. Note that pile sources legitimately sit outside
      the project root (ancestor CLAUDE.md, the harness config dir), so those
      need a sensible fallback rather than a long ../../.. chain.
   3. A clustered conflict line still spells out up to five near-identical prose
      objects (~700 bytes). Per-object truncation would help, though
      :admission-dedup removes the cause.
   4. Remedy strings name CLI verbs as string literals; one shipped naming
      `claim compile`, which does not exist (the verb is `compile-context`),
      and three tests asserted the wrong string without noticing. Assert
      suggested commands against the actual verb table."
  :evidence
  ["Shipped and fixed 2026-08-08: `claim compile --budget` -> `claim compile-context --budget`"]}}
